WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.533 --> 00:00:13.097
Hi, Little Fox readers!

2
00:00:13.497 --> 00:00:14.658
It's me, Mellissa, again.

3
00:00:15.499 --> 00:00:19.261
My little brother just asked me to take a picture of his plastic army soldiers.

4
00:00:20.061 --> 00:00:22.803
He has them lined up outside in straight long rows.

5
00:00:23.703 --> 00:00:25.785
They look kind of like the cool landmark Mr.

6
00:00:25.845 --> 00:00:31.448
Fay told us about this week: the Terra-cotta Army in China's Shaanxi province.

7
00:00:32.448 --> 00:00:36.189
The Terra-cotta soldiers are a lot bigger than my brother's plastic toys, though.

8
00:00:36.929 --> 00:00:39.210
They're life-size and made of clay.

9
00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:47.492
In fact, this ancient massive army is considered one of the world's greatest archaeological discoveries and was even

10
00:00:47.532 --> 00:00:50.913
declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987.

11
00:00:52.413 --> 00:00:57.515
The Terra-cotta Army was created during the reign of Emperor Qin Shihuangdi.

12
00:00:57.535 --> 00:01:06.673
In 221 B.C., Qin Shihuangdi—who was just a king at the time—managed to conquer and unify China's warring

13
00:01:06.733 --> 00:01:10.074
states and proclaim himself China's first emperor.

14
00:01:11.055 --> 00:01:16.276
He was a brutal leader who had lots of people executed and trusted almost no one.

15
00:01:17.137 --> 00:01:19.657
But he also accomplished some pretty impressive things.

16
00:01:20.598 --> 00:01:28.280
He standardized the area's weights and measures, laws, written language, weapons—even the length of axles on carts!

17
00:01:29.424 --> 00:01:36.106
Under his orders new fortresses were constructed, along with a series of defensive walls that eventually became the

18
00:01:36.186 --> 00:01:37.546
Great Wall of China.

19
00:01:38.447 --> 00:01:43.628
In addition, he instructed workers to build an underground funerary compound for himself.

20
00:01:44.548 --> 00:01:46.569
That's where he would be buried after death.

21
00:01:48.189 --> 00:01:54.491
Mr. Fay told us that Qin Shihuangdi's tomb was huge—about fifty square kilometers!

22
00:01:55.531 --> 00:02:02.818
According to a famous historian, it contained a small-scale model of the world with miniature palaces, trees carved

23
00:02:02.838 --> 00:02:06.199
from jade, even flowing rivers and an ocean.

24
00:02:07.319 --> 00:02:12.601
Mercury was used for the water, and pearls were attached to the ceiling to represent stars.

25
00:02:13.661 --> 00:02:20.163
In order to protect the tomb from robbers, workers rigged crossbows that would automatically fire if anyone entered.

26
00:02:21.183 --> 00:02:27.850
Before Qin Shihuangdi's reign, it was customary for a ruler's entire royal court to be slaughtered and buried

27
00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:31.492
with him, so that they could continue to serve him in the afterlife.

28
00:02:32.572 --> 00:02:39.295
However, this practice had long been abandoned by the emperor's time, so he decided to use clay statues

29
00:02:39.335 --> 00:02:47.038
instead. Qin Shihuangdi also ordered his workers to build a clay army that could guard his tomb after

30
00:02:47.078 --> 00:02:50.580
he died—this became the Terra-cotta army.

31
00:02:51.560 --> 00:03:01.208
The army includes a huge assortment of soldiers—at least eight thousand infantry, cavalry, archers, officers—all in battle formation

32
00:03:01.528 --> 00:03:03.649
and organized according to military rank.

33
00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:11.335
The statues' bodies are pretty similar, but each warrior has unique details such as hats, mustaches, shoes, and

34
00:03:11.375 --> 00:03:17.340
hair. While some of the warriors look fierce and ready for battle, others appear pretty calm.

35
00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:23.164
They were originally armed with real bronze weapons like swords, spears, and axes.

36
00:03:24.245 --> 00:03:28.549
Some are even crouching, ready to fire the working crossbows they once held.

37
00:03:29.449 --> 00:03:33.393
In addition to the warriors, there are also life-size horses and chariots.

38
00:03:35.074 --> 00:03:38.957
Some scholars have assumed that the statues are portraits of actual soldiers.

39
00:03:39.918 --> 00:03:46.443
It's more likely, however, that they don't represent specific people, but rather, all the various ethnic groups in

40
00:03:46.483 --> 00:03:53.754
China. Approximately one thousand craftsmen worked for 11 years to make the statues from terra-cotta, a type of

41
00:03:53.814 --> 00:04:00.395
clay. All the soldiers' legs were cast in similar molds, but for the upper bodies and arms, different

42
00:04:00.435 --> 00:04:04.156
types of molds were used, to vary the soldiers' head and body shapes.

43
00:04:05.337 --> 00:04:10.978
After the body sections were pulled out of the molds, expert sculptors refined them, adding all sorts of

44
00:04:11.018 --> 00:04:14.399
minute details to make the statues look unique and lifelike.

45
00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:20.590
And get this: every warrior has the name of the sculptor who made it.

46
00:04:21.531 --> 00:04:24.773
At first I assumed the artists were proud of their work and wanted to sign it.

47
00:04:25.574 --> 00:04:26.014
But Mr.

48
00:04:26.094 --> 00:04:29.777
Fay said they really did it so their work could be checked for quality.

49
00:04:30.677 --> 00:04:37.182
The emperor insisted that any statue that wasn't perfect had to be redone, or worse, he would have

50
00:04:37.222 --> 00:04:39.984
the sculptor—gulp—executed!

51
00:04:40.384 --> 00:04:41.585
Talk about pressure!

52
00:04:42.585 --> 00:04:47.452
After all the carving was finished, the statues were dried in a kiln, and then coated in a

53
00:04:47.592 --> 00:04:48.893
thick layer of lacquer.

54
00:04:49.873 --> 00:04:52.814
Next workers painted them in rich, bright colors.

55
00:04:53.834 --> 00:04:59.436
The finished statues were carried down ramps to narrow underground halls, where they were put into position.

56
00:05:00.356 --> 00:05:02.877
Then the halls were sealed off and hidden from view.

57
00:05:04.417 --> 00:05:08.258
For over two thousand years, nobody saw the Terra-cotta Army.

58
00:05:09.198 --> 00:05:14.720
In fact, it wasn't mentioned in historical texts, so nobody even knew it existed.

59
00:05:15.764 --> 00:05:21.970
Then in 1974 some peasants stumbled across fragments of a clay statue in the ground while digging a

60
00:05:22.010 --> 00:05:30.958
well. Government archaeologists began excavating the site, and discovered an enormous underground pit, containing thousands of the clay

61
00:05:31.018 --> 00:05:36.823
soldiers. Eventually more pits were found, each containing many soldiers and horses.

62
00:05:37.884 --> 00:05:42.428
Excavation has continued at the site ever since the discovery of the Terra-cotta Army.

63
00:05:43.336 --> 00:05:48.820
The first three pits that were found have been enclosed in large buildings and are now the Museum

64
00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:50.161
of the Terracotta Army.

65
00:05:51.122 --> 00:05:55.626
What's really cool about this museum is that it's still a functioning archaeological site.

66
00:05:56.466 --> 00:06:00.549
So visitors to the museum can actually see the excavation process.

67
00:06:02.171 --> 00:06:06.394
Unfortunately it hasn't been easy to safely dig up the Terra-cotta Army.

68
00:06:07.294 --> 00:06:12.621
When a statue is exposed to the air, the lacquer on its surface immediately shrinks and peels away

69
00:06:12.661 --> 00:06:14.862
from the clay, taking the pigment with it.

70
00:06:15.742 --> 00:06:22.384
Archaeologists unearthing these statues sometimes have just minutes to try to preserve the color before it's gone forever.

71
00:06:23.324 --> 00:06:31.526
Because of this and other difficulties, the excavation has been a very slow process; as of 2012 archaeologists

72
00:06:31.586 --> 00:06:36.047
hadn't done any excavation of the tomb itself—they're afraid they might damage it.

73
00:06:36.928 --> 00:06:38.388
Well, I have to get going now.

74
00:06:39.328 --> 00:06:42.871
My brother wants me to take that picture of his army before he knocks it down with his

75
00:06:42.911 --> 00:06:44.192
remote control army tank!

76
00:06:45.153 --> 00:06:46.054
See you next week!
